 ],
 "digest": "This week's digest traces competing pathways of financial subordination and autonomy across the Global South. Ghana's 1970s debt refusal and the emergence of South-South public banking for climate finance in *International Journal of Political Economy* anchor the longer histories through which Southern actors have resisted Northern financial dominance. Sustainable debt taxonomies reshaping Latin American capital markets, alongside regulatory contests over stablecoins, CBDCs, and monetary sovereignty in emerging economies, reveal how financial devices themselves become sites of struggle over autonomy and dependency. We also track institutional voices—Absa's critique of sovereign lending patterns, central bank officials discussing payments infrastructure and system design—that expose the professional networks and policy disputes animating financial centres from Johannesburg to Ho Chi Minh City."
}